page guide_recording {
  title = "Console recording"
  chapter_header "Console recording" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "8 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-21"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
A console recording is a video of one VM's display, written by the lab daemon
to an MP4 or WebM file while the VM runs. You start one by hand with
[vmlab record](cmd_record), ask for one alongside a boot or a script run with
`--record`, or start one from a script with `Machine.record`. The recorder
reads the same VNC socket [vmlab console](cmd_console) and the screenshot
methods use, so a recording needs nothing in the guest and works before the
guest has an agent. This chapter explains what ends up in the file, how the
pointer gets there, what idle compression and the timeline do, who owns a
recording, and what a recording cannot do. The flags are in
[vmlab record](cmd_record) and the method in
[wscript API: Machine](ref_ws_machine).
DOC

  h2 "What a recording holds"

  p <<'DOC'
The recorder holds one connection to the VM's VNC socket for the life of the
recording, receives every framebuffer update and QEMU's cursor shape, and
writes constant-rate frames to the host's `ffmpeg`. The output path's
extension picks the format: `.mp4` is H.264 in `yuv420p`, `.webm` is VP9.
Nothing else is accepted, `.gif` included, and the refusal comes before any
socket is opened. Convert the MP4 afterwards for anything else.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The video runs at 30 frames per second in a 1920x1080 frame unless `--fps` or
`--size` says otherwise. Every source frame is scaled into that one size with
its aspect ratio kept and letterboxed, so a 640x480 firmware screen and the
1280x1024 desktop the guest switches to later land in the same file at the
same size, with bars where the shapes differ. A size must have even
dimensions, because 4:2:0 video cannot hold an odd edge, and each dimension
must lie between 16 and 7680x4320. The rate must lie between 1 and 120.
A frame is emitted at the end of every interval whether or not anything
arrived, so a still screen repeats its last picture rather than stalling the
clock.
DOC

  p "There is no audio and there are no captions."

  h2 "The pointer rule"

  p <<'DOC'
QEMU never sends a pointer position over VNC, so the recorder has no way to
learn where the guest's pointer is. It draws the pointer at the last position
vmlab itself sent: a `mouse_move`, `mouse_click` or `mouse_drag` from a script,
or `vmlab vm mouse-move`, `click` and `drag` from the shell. A person moving
the mouse in a viewer opened by `vmlab console` is not tracked, and the drawn
pointer stays where vmlab last put it.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The pointer is drawn only when QEMU sends a cursor shape, which it does for a
guest on a virtio-vga, virtio-gpu, qxl or vmware display. On std VGA, bochs
and cirrus the guest paints its own pointer into the framebuffer, QEMU sends
no shape, and the recorder draws nothing, so a pointer never appears twice.
The shape is the guest's own: a Windows arrow looks like a Windows arrow. A
virtio-gpu guest that hides its cursor, as a full-screen video player does,
still shows the last shape QEMU sent, because QEMU does not report the hide.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Across a guest reboot the pointer disappears and comes back. QEMU never
clears a cursor shape; it re-announces the last one, with its hotspot zeroed,
when the display resets and again with every resolution change through the
boot. The recorder recognises that repeat and hides the pointer instead of
drawing the old desktop's arrow over the firmware, the kernel's text and the
login screen; the pointer returns once the greeter or the desktop defines a
shape of its own. The same happens when a running desktop changes resolution:
the pointer is hidden for the moment until the compositor defines its cursor
again. A cursor whose own hotspot is at (0,0) cannot be told from QEMU's
repeat of it, so after a reset it stays hidden until the guest sends a
different shape.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Every click vmlab sends, and both ends of a drag, gets an amber ripple at the
click point that grows and fades over half a second. The ripple is drawn
whether or not a cursor shape is known, so a click on a std VGA guest is
still visible in the video, and it is drawn beneath the pointer, so the
pointer keeps its colours while the ripple plays.
DOC

  h2 "Idle compression"

  p <<'DOC'
A boot or an install spends most of its time on a screen that is not
changing. `--compress-idle` cuts those stretches out. The recorder watches
each frame interval: an interval is idle when the framebuffer rectangles it
received cover no more than 64x64 pixels in total and vmlab sent no input
during it. A blinking caret or a clock with seconds does not break an idle
run, because QEMU reports dirt in 16-pixel cells and such a change stays well
under the area. A run of idle intervals longer than the threshold, 2 seconds
unless `--compress-idle=SECS` names another, becomes half a second of video.
A click on a static screen is never cut, because the interval it lands in is
not idle.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Without the flag nothing is cut and the video is as long as the recording
was. With it the video is shorter than the wall clock, and the timeline, if
you asked for one, records where the cuts fell so you can map a moment in the
video back to when it happened.
DOC

  h2 "The timeline"

  p <<'DOC'
`--timeline` writes a JSON sidecar beside the video, `<output>.events.json`,
listing every input vmlab sent during the recording. Each entry carries `at`,
the second of the output video it falls on after any idle cuts, `wall`, the
seconds since the recording started on the wall clock, a `kind`, and the
fields that kind has:
DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Kind" | "Fields" | "Sent by" |
      | "`keys`" | "`chord`" | "`send_keys`, `vmlab vm sendkeys`" |
      | "`text`" | "`text`" | "`type_text`, `type_text_paced`" |
      | "`move`" | "`x`, `y`" | "`mouse_move`, `vmlab vm mouse-move`" |
      | "`click`" | "`button`, `x`, `y` (position only when the click moved first)" | "`mouse_click`, `vmlab vm click`" |
      | "`drag`" | "`x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`" | "`mouse_drag`, `vmlab vm drag`" |
  }

  p <<'DOC'
The file as a whole carries `video`, the file name of the recording, `fps`, `size`
and `duration`, the `events` list, and `cuts`: one `{wall_start, wall_end,
kept}` per idle stretch that was shortened, each in wall-clock seconds with
the length it was cut to. A recording made without idle compression has an
empty `cuts` list and `at` equal to `wall` on every entry. Like the pointer,
the timeline knows only what vmlab sent; a person typing into a viewer leaves
no entry.
DOC

  code json { source = <<'SRC'
{
  "video": "win01-20261021T101500.000.mp4",
  "fps": 30,
  "size": "1920x1080",
  "duration": 41.5,
  "events": [
    {"at": 3.2, "wall": 3.2, "kind": "keys", "chord": "ctrl-alt-del"},
    {"at": 9.8, "wall": 31.1, "kind": "text", "text": "Password1!\n"},
    {"at": 12.0, "wall": 33.3, "kind": "click", "button": "left", "x": 640, "y": 410}
  ],
  "cuts": [
    {"wall_start": 4.0, "wall_end": 25.8, "kept": 0.5}
  ]
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Who owns a recording"

  p <<'DOC'
The lab daemon owns every recording, not the CLI that asked for it. A
recording started with `vmlab record start` carries on after the command
returns, after the terminal closes, and until something ends it: a
`vmlab record stop`, its `--max-duration`, or the machine going away. The
daemon finalises every recording to a playable file when the machine is
stopped by `vmlab vm stop`, `vmlab down`, `vmlab destroy` or `vmlab vm
destroy`, and when the daemon itself exits. The `host.disk_low` watchdog
finalises every live recording too, so a full disk leaves you with playable
files rather than truncated ones.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A guest reboot does not end a recording: the VNC socket stays and the frames
keep coming. Neither does `vmlab vm restart` nor a snapshot restore, both of
which replace the QEMU process under the display. The recorder holds the
last frame across the gap and reconnects to the new QEMU's socket, so the
video shows the old screen, then the new boot, with no break in the file.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`--record <vm>=<path>` on [vmlab up](cmd_up), `vmlab vm start` and
`vmlab vm restart` records a boot from its first frame: QEMU starts paused,
the recorder attaches, and only then does the VM continue, so the file opens
on the firmware's first screen. Every `--record` is checked before anything
boots, and one refusal arms none. A `--record` naming a VM that was already
running, so that nothing booted, records it from now; one naming a VM that
failed to start fails the verb with `machine X did not start, so its
recording was not started`. On [vmlab script](cmd_script) the recordings last
exactly the run: they start before the script and are finalised when it
returns, whether it succeeded or failed.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A script's own `m.record(path, opts)` returns a `Recording` handle whose
`stop()` finalises the file and returns its path. A recording the script
never stops is finalised by the daemon when the run ends, success or failure,
so a script that fails halfway still leaves a playable file of how it got
there. One recording per machine: a second `start` while one is live is a
`conflict` that names the live file.
DOC

  h2 "What a recording needs from the host"

  p <<'DOC'
Recording needs `ffmpeg` on the host with the encoder for the format you
asked for: `libx264` for `.mp4`, `libvpx-vp9` for `.webm`. vmlab looks for
`ffmpeg` on `PATH`, or at the path the `ffmpeg` key in the
[host config](ref_host) names, and runs `ffmpeg -encoders` to see what it
can write. The check happens when a recording starts, and nowhere else: a
host without ffmpeg runs labs as before, and `vmlab up` never looks for it
unless `--record` is given. A missing binary or encoder is a `failed` refusal
that names the host config key, `PATH` and the encoder it needed.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A recording named with no path lands in the lab's own `.vmlab/recordings/`
as `<vm>-<UTC timestamp>.mp4`, beside `screenshots/` (see
[Files and directories](ref_paths)). A relative `-o` path on the CLI resolves
against the shell's working directory; a relative path from a script resolves
against the lab root.
DOC

  h2 "Watching one"

  p <<'DOC'
[vmlab status](cmd_status) prints a `RECORDING` section, with the machine,
elapsed time and path, whenever any recording is live, and `-v` adds
`recording=<path>` to a recording machine's detail line. `vmlab record
status` lists the same with the options in force. Every start emits
`record.started` and every finish `record.stopped`, with the length of the
video and why it ended (see [Events](ref_events)), so a handler can pick up
a finished file.
DOC

  h2 "Limits"

  list {
    li "No GIF. Convert the MP4 afterwards with ffmpeg."
    li "No audio."
    li "A container has no display, so it cannot be recorded: `unsupported`, with `machine X has no display`."
    li "A person's pointer in a VNC viewer is not tracked; only input vmlab sent moves the drawn pointer or appears in the timeline."
    li "A virtio-gpu guest that hides its cursor still shows the last cursor shape QEMU sent."
    li "Template build VMs cannot be recorded."
  }

  callout "Record the boot, not the wait" {
    kind = :tip
    body = "A Windows install under `--record` with `--compress-idle` is a few minutes of video for an hour of wall clock, and the timeline says where each minute came from. Leave compression off when the length of a pause is the thing you are measuring."
  }
}
